WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.354
Secure firmware starts before the main
application runs.

2
00:00:03.434 --> 00:00:04.191
At a high level,

3
00:00:04.271 --> 00:00:08.465
startup should establish that the device
is running authorized firmware and that the

4
00:00:08.545 --> 00:00:10.583
state needed for safe operation is valid.

5
00:00:10.663 --> 00:00:13.517
The details depend on hardware and product
requirements,

6
00:00:13.597 --> 00:00:15.519
but the design question is consistent:

7
00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:17.987
what is checked before the device trusts
itself?

8
00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:21.096
Signed firmware concepts support
authorization decisions.

9
00:00:21.176 --> 00:00:25.253
The device can use a protected trust
anchor and metadata policy to decide whether an

10
00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:26.498
image is allowed to run.

11
00:00:26.578 --> 00:00:29.490
This course does not implement
cryptography or boot logic;

12
00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:33.589
it focuses on the review questions C
developers need to understand when their code

13
00:00:33.669 --> 00:00:35.183
depends on startup integrity.

14
00:00:35.263 --> 00:00:37.884
Startup state includes more than the
firmware image.

15
00:00:37.964 --> 00:00:40.993
Configuration records, version markers,
calibration data,

16
00:00:41.073 --> 00:00:43.869
endpoint settings, identity material,
rollback state,

17
00:00:43.949 --> 00:00:46.861
and factory provisioning data may all
influence behavior.

18
00:00:46.941 --> 00:00:50.960
The device should validate required state
before using it and define what happens

19
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.136
when the state is missing or inconsistent.

20
00:00:53.216 --> 00:00:55.139
Failure behavior must be predictable.

21
00:00:55.219 --> 00:00:58.072
If firmware validation fails,
configuration is damaged,

22
00:00:58.152 --> 00:01:01.589
version policy rejects an image, or
recovery metadata is incomplete,

23
00:01:01.669 --> 00:01:04.348
the device should not quietly enter normal
operation.

24
00:01:04.428 --> 00:01:07.690
Depending on the product, it may enter a
controlled recovery mode,

25
00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.798
continue with a previous valid image,
report a bounded fault,

26
00:01:10.878 --> 00:01:11.985
or stop in a safe state.

27
00:01:12.065 --> 00:01:15.967
Boot security depends on protected trust
anchors and reviewed configuration.

28
00:01:16.047 --> 00:01:19.892
Hardware option settings, debug
configuration, storage layout, boot order,

29
00:01:19.972 --> 00:01:23.874
recovery permissions, and manufacturing
choices can change the meaning of the

30
00:01:23.954 --> 00:01:24.362
design.

31
00:01:24.442 --> 00:01:28.227
These settings should be treated as
release artifacts and verified as part of

32
00:01:28.307 --> 00:01:29.472
production readiness.

33
00:01:29.552 --> 00:01:31.940
Testing should include negative startup
cases.

34
00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.874
Teams should exercise invalid images,
missing metadata,

35
00:01:34.954 --> 00:01:38.158
bad configuration, interrupted updates,
reset during startup,

36
00:01:38.238 --> 00:01:40.568
and recovery decisions on target-like
builds.

37
00:01:40.648 --> 00:01:44.725
A startup design is not complete until
both successful and failed validation paths

38
00:01:44.805 --> 00:01:45.620
are understood.
